  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Janette Price)",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This case involves a gang-related murder in which a jury found the defendant guilty beyond a reasonable doubt. There was one trial conducted, and the defendant was sentenced to life in prison. The Sixth Circuit Court of Appeals erred in its conclusion that the Michigan Supreme Court's decision on double jeopardy was unreasonable. The AEDPA precludes habeas corpus relief where a State court makes reasonable factual determinations. The Michigan Supreme Court's decision was not only reasonable, but also correct."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: When you -- when you say this was a factual matter, I got the impression that as to basic facts, there really wasn't any dispute about them."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, that's sort of a special situation because in those situations you could never get the answer if you always decided it on -- on immunity grounds. I mean --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. LAMKEN: That -- that --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0002-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "He tells the clerk, put in a new docket entry, order withdrawn, motion to dismiss denied."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, being -- being revisited on appeal is different from being revisited at the trial by the trial judge. That's -- that's the distinction that's being drawn by your opponent here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MORAN: Because what -- what AEDPA requires is not to look at the decisions of the lower courts, but to look to see whether the decision of the Michigan Supreme Court was a clearly unreasonable application."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MORAN: Excuse me, Justice Ginsburg. You're quite correct. He said, I granted a motion. I didn't direct a verdict."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0003-0000",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That standard was met here. The Michigan Supreme Court itself acknowledged that a docket entry is exactly the sort of formality that they were looking for but then inexplicably failed to notice that there had been such a docket entry made in this case. Not inexplicably the State failed to include the docket entry in its appendix in violation of the Michigan court rules. That's why the Michigan Supreme Court was apparently unaware of the dispositive docket entry."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Wasn't that called to their attention in rehearing, though?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: How do you explain their failure to grant rehearing?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: That's not a discretionary matter, I wouldn't think, in a criminal case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0003-0009",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "reconsideration filed, yes, Justice Stevens."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0003-0011",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "The Michigan Supreme Court itself acknowledged that a docket entry is exactly the sort of formality that they were looking for"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Even when there was an error of law called to their attention."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0003-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: May I ask you another question? Assume it would have been an acquittal as a matter of Michigan law because of the -- the docket entry. Would it necessarily follow that it was also an acquittal for purposes of Federal law?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0003-0010",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It was a 5 to 2 vote for denial of reconsideration."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0003-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: So your syllogism is that if he was acquitted as a matter of Michigan law, a fortiori the Double Jeopardy Clause applies as a matter of Federal law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0003-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BUSCH: Thank you, Your Honor. The respondent here has stated that the Michigan Supreme Court agrees with his position with respect to the second prong of AEDPA."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2002-02-524",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c21e2ad6ac90d01fc353781aff591c1626630a2180890a4c752ad64ff4c5b119",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c21e2ad6ac90-0003-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are -- he has cited several cases and we have several cases in the other direction, interestingly enough, including one, United States versus Baggett, which comes out of the Sixth Circuit itself, which says -- in that case there were -- three times the judge announced a ruling and then agreed to hold it in abeyance. In -- in that case the Court said that -- that -- the appeal court said that they were free to change their mind -- the judge was free to change their mind any time prior to the entry of judgment."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
